-- title:
--   Smooth locus in the p-fibre: complement of the crossings
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $\mathfrak X$ be a `DRModelPackage p`, i.e. a bundle of data and properties for the two-chart integral model `DRModel p` over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$ — the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebras of the full modular function field generated by $j$ and by $j^{-1}$, with structure morphism `DRModel.toBase p` — consisting of properness, flatness and integrality of that model, integral closedness of its sections over affine opens, curve models of the function field over $\mathbb Q$ and over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ identified with the corresponding base changes together with Galois and place compatibilities, two sections `εinf`, `εzero` over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z$, a distinguished open `smoothLocus` whose structure morphism is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and which contains every open with smooth structure morphism, and further data, among them morphisms `compInf` and `compZero` attached to a field. Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and let $y$ be a point of the pullback of `DRModel.toBase p` along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb Z \to \kappa$. Then $y$ lies in the preimage of $\mathfrak X$'s `smoothLocus` under the first projection of that pullback if and only if it is not the case that $y$ lies both in the range of the underlying map of $\mathfrak X$`.compInf κ` and in the range of the underlying map of $\mathfrak X$`.compZero κ`.
--
--   This identifies, inside the geometric fibre at $p$ of the Deligne–Rapoport model, the locus where the model is smooth over the base: it is exactly the complement of the set of points lying on both distinguished components, i.e. of the crossing points. It is used for the statement that the base change of the generic point lands in the smooth locus, for the computation of the branch ideals of the two components at a crossing, and in the construction of the resolved model packages and their charts.
